<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial Abbey of Essen</h1><p class="st-meta">852 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Essen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A royal convent for noble women whose princess-abbesses ruled the town of Essen and whose treasure includes the oldest surviving statue of the Virgin Mary.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen">Essen</a> Abbey was founded around 845 to 852 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altfrid">Altfrid</a>, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a>, as a community of canonesses for women of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> nobility. It received royal protection and immunity, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottonian-dynasty">Ottonians</a> it became a favoured house of the ruling dynasty.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The abbesses <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathilde-abbess-of-essen">Mathilde</a>, a granddaughter of Otto I, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theophanu-abbess-of-essen">Theophanu</a>, granddaughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Otto II</a>, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen">Essen</a> a centre of Ottonian art; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-madonna-of-essen">Golden Madonna</a> of about 980 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-mathilde-cross">Otto-Mathilde Cross</a> belong to its treasury. The abbess was a princess of the Empire with a seat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a>, and ruled the small territory around Essen, often in conflict with the town.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen">Essen</a> was secularised in 1803 and passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>; the last abbess was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-kunigunde-of-saxony">Maria Kunigunde of Saxony</a>. The town grew into the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a> industry, and the former abbey church became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen-cathedral">Essen Cathedral</a> when the diocese was founded in 1958.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/imperial-abbey-of-essen.jpg" alt="Imperial Abbey of Essen" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Ralf Hüls · CC BY-SA 2.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:M%C3%BCnster_Essen_Kreuzgang_20060326.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>852 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Essen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mathilde, Theophanu, Maria Kunigunde of Saxony</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Saxon, Middle Low German, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Essen Abbey, Stift Essen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1328&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-essen" aria-label="Imperial Abbey of Essen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4839 396 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imperial Abbey of Essen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5159 652L5229 694M5159 652L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5159 652h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5159 652h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1328&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-essen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
